[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Storage Engine and Page Layout (Heap Tuples) in PostgreSQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how PostgreSQL stores data on disk is essential for database administrators and backend engineers tuning performance. At the core of PostgreSQL's storage architecture is the relation data file, which is divided into fixed-size blocks called pages or blocks, typically 8KB in size."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Anatomy of a PostgreSQL Page"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every 8KB page follows a standard internal layout. It is not just a raw stream of rows; instead, it is structured to allow concurrent access, efficient tuple location, and crash recovery. The page layout consists of the following components:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Page Header (PageHeaderData): Occupies the beginning of the page (typically 24 bytes) and contains metadata such as free space pointers, page flags, and transaction visibility info."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Line Pointers (ItemIds): An array of pointers (each 4 bytes) that point to the actual tuples. These act as an indirection layer, known as offsets."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Free Space: Unallocated space between the line pointers growing downward and the actual tuple data growing upward."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Heap Tuples (Items): The actual row data, stacked from the end of the page moving backward toward the free space."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Heap Tuple Structure (Tuple Header + Data)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A heap tuple is composed of a fixed-size header (HeapTupleHeaderData, typically 23 bytes), an optional NULL bitmap, an optional OID, and the actual user data attributes. The tuple header stores vital MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) metadata."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"t_xmin: The transaction ID that inserted the row."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"t_xmax: The transaction ID that deleted or updated the row (0 if active)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"t_cid: Command identifier (index of the command within the transaction)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"t_ctid: The physical location (Tuple ID) of the current or updated version of the tuple, consisting of a page number and line pointer index."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Inspecting Page Layout with pageinspect"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"PostgreSQL provides the extension 'pageinspect' to inspect database pages directly at a low level. You can use it to examine the page header and line pointers of a table."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"CREATE EXTENSION pageinspect;

-- Inspect the first page (block 0) of a target table
SELECT * FROM heap_page_items(get_raw_page('your_table_name', 0));"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Implications for Performance and Bloat"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because PostgreSQL updates rows by writing a completely new tuple (INSERT + DELETE under the hood for MVCC), pages accumulate dead tuples over time. When updates are frequent, free space fragments, leading to table bloat. Regular execution of VACUUM reclaims this free space by making line pointers available for reuse, though it does not inherently return disk space to the operating system unless processed with VACUUM FULL."}]
